For every abstract that contains a sentence relevant to this claim, return one record:
- pmid;
- quote: one or two consecutive sentences copied exactly, character for character, from that abstract;
- quote_role: finding (a result of this study) | background (stated as known, cited, or a review summarising others) | speculation (may, might, could, suggests, we propose);
- stance: supports | refutes | neither (for this claim, in this context). "refutes" only when the finding says the relation itself does not hold (no effect, or the opposite effect); a finding about consequences, side effects or other relations of the same entities is "neither";
- species: the organism the quoted sentence is about (a sentence about other organisms does not support a claim about this one);
- endpoint: what the finding measures: lifespan | healthspan | biomarker | disease | mechanism | unknown;
- system: in_vivo | ex_vivo | in_vitro; study_type: primary_experiment | rct | open_label_pilot | observational | review | other;
- sexes: list of male/female in which the effect was found (empty if not stated);
- effect_percent: % change of lifespan if the abstract states it, else null;
- qualifiers_match: for each of species, sex, age_at_start, dose, comparator, population: "yes" (matches), "no" (contradicts, e.g. a disease population for healthy adults, ex vivo for an in vivo claim) or "n/a" (the claim does not specify it, or the abstract does not say);
- intervention_match: exact | dose_differs | regimen_differs | related_compound | class;
- program: "ITP" or "CITP" if the study is from such a multi-site program, else "";
- flags: any of conflict_of_interest, small_n, open_label, subgroup_only, preprint, short_lived_controls.
Trials and studies with several outcomes: judge the claim by the primary outcome. If the primary outcome was null and only secondary outcomes or subgroups improved, return two records from that abstract: the null primary outcome with stance "refutes", and the positive secondary or subgroup result with stance "supports" and flag "subgroup_only".
Skip abstracts with nothing relevant. Return an empty list if none is relevant. Do not use knowledge outside these abstracts.

